I've been trying to ascend a tourist, and finally managed an offline
ascension. Mia was just a natural at making friends: on dlvl1, she
rubbed an unID'd lamp and freed a grateful Djinn. At 10HP, options are
limited, but Mia settled on MagicBane.

Mia then proceeded directly into the Gnomish Mines, making every
square she stood on - and every chokepoint -
an E-square. She chucked darts at enemies, then daggers wrested from
fallen gnomes and dwarves, building
up skill until she reached Minetown. Minetown had a coaligned altar,
so Mia camped, killing every passing gnome
until she had arranged a sizeable stack of armor (twenty pairs of iron
shoes and no mithril!), and
the Frost Brand and Mjollinr artifacts. She price-ID'd the shoes and
helms and triumphantly grasped a +4 orcish helm and +2 iron shoes. She
then ventured deeper into the mines to find a dwarvish mithril coat,
and returned to the surface with -6AC.

Sokoban was interrupted by an unruly giant who dropped a boulder in
the wrong place at the wrong time.
Mia had absentmindedly left her stupid pick-axe in minetown and made a
very long trek back and forth. During this
trek, her luck ran even worse, as she destroyed her Gauntlets of
Dexterity with an unID'd Destroy Armor scroll,
and lost all her gold by reading a difficult spellbook.

She finally conquered the treasure zoo and returned with a bag of
holding. She then switched from MagicBane to a dual wielding of Frost
Brand and a long sword. The rest of the dungeon wasn't so bad. There
were several anthills, allowing Mia to gain fire resistance from fire
ants, and several beehives, allowing her to finish upgrading her
strength. Best of all was a throne room containing yellow, blue and
silver dragons. Mia had burned all her scrolls of identify on
identifying *rings* and had a ring of conflict in her posession, so
she slipped it on and watched everything kill everything else. When
the dust cleared, she was the proud owner of silver dragon scales.
After finding a magic marker at approx. dlvl 26, she was the proud
owner of silver dragon scale *mail*.

Enter the quest.

My goodness, the tourist quest *more* than compensates for the
tourist's pitiful starting equipment. Something like
five general stores, seven barracks, a brothel full of incubi and
succubi... wow, the master of thieves sure knew
how to live it up. ;) And here Mia was, supposed to restore order to
her fallen hometown, and she was basically
looting it. Quite noble of her, I must say, and she quite appreciated
the magic harp and wands of wishing and death
she found in the process of retrieving the town's sacred credit card.

Mia had been played as a weak combat wombat, and with a wand of
wishing, her playstyle shifted towards spellcasting. Her wishes, in no
particular order, were

1. eye of the aethiopica
2. helm of brilliance
3. spellbook of identify
4. blessed wand of polymorph
5. (wrested, since wand was 0:2) blessed figurine of an archon.

Whoa, that Archon kicked some butt. Since Mia had a magic harp and
could tame minotaurs and purple worms, wish 5 wasn't that necessary,
but he came with Sunsword and a shield of reflection so I couldn't
really complain. Anyway, Mia started casting Identify, poly'd for a
spellbook of magic mapping, found a spellbook of jumping (AWESOME!),
and became a spellcasting force to reckon with. And with dual wielding
of +6 long sword and a +7 frost brand, she felled minotaurs and mind
flayers in three turns (although she preferred making friends with
minotaurs using her magic harp :) )

6. (castle's wand) speed boots
7. (Castle's wand) ring of levitation, somehow lost my old one by
Jubilex's swamp. :(

Gehennom wasn't that eventful. Once a tourist has the PYEC artifact,
*nothing* else is that eventful. My archon died, and I have *NO CLUE*
how it happened, because I'd genocided all liches. Perhaps he ran into
some nasy spellcaster such as a nalfeshnee, I dunno. At least he
dropped Sunsword. The biggest event was waking the Wizard of Yendor
before finding Vlad's lair. That was stupid. And even though I knew
magic mapping, finding the stairs took forever because they were
obscured by some other object. Even worse, I got my brains sucked by a
mind flayer in the Sanctum and suffered amnesia, so I had to cast
magic mapping about 30 times, ending at the uneventful (once again,
correct altar on first try) Astral Plane.

